  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure slides overhead tonight allowing for strong \nradiational cooing across the area. With Mt. Washington's \nobserved dewpoint sitting at the freezing mark early this \nafternoon, it is not out of the question for some the more \nsheltered northern valley locations to realize this as a low \ntemperature by early Saturday morning. I don't believe any \nfrost/freeze headlines are warranted here as any occurrences of \neither should be limited in scope, but certainly anyone with \nsensitive crops or plants that live in the north should take \nprotective measures as temperatures are expected to drop into at\nleast the mid-30s. Patchy valley fog is likely as well. South \nof the mountains will stay warmer, but not by much as the \nfoothills and better radiating locations fall into the upper \n30s. The coastal plain and southern New Hampshire stay the \nwarmest with temperatures in the 40s.\n\nAs high pressure slides off to the east Saturday, upper ridging\ntakes hold and the resulting southwesterly flow should help \ntemperatures climb a few degrees higher than what we are seeing \nout there today. It will be more noticeable in the north as \ntemperatures climb into the low to mid-70, with valley locations\nclimbing into the upper 70s with downsloping winds. The coastal\nplain will end up pretty similar to today as the seabreeze will\nbe able to come ashore and keep those areas in the low 70s. \nOverall, clear skies and dry air will make for a pretty \nquintessential fall day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nRidging aloft over the Northeast will give way to broad \ntroughing Saturday night through Monday. Embedded waves within \nthe trough will spread forcing for ascent over the area for rain\nchances while the disconnect between waves will limit the \nchances for continuous rainfall through the period.\n\nThe first primary wave will slide across the Mid Atlantic \nSaturday night and then south of New England Sunday. This wave \nwill advect deep moisture into the region with ensembles showing\nmean PWATs of 1.5 to 2 inches pushing into areas south of the \nmountains by Sunday morning. A majority of ensemble (mainly GEFS\nand CMC) members suggest a compact area of low pressure will \ntrack across southern New England and will focus the heaviest of\nrainfall south of the area with a sharp drop off in QPF to the \nnorth. There are some members that bring at least moderate \nrainfall in to southern NH and far SW Maine with a sharp cut off\nin QPF near the foothills. The ECMWF camp is a bit different in\nthat several members do not close off a surface low allowing \nlight to moderate rainfall to spread north into the mountains. \nRegardless of the range of solutions there are no flood concerns\nwith the main focus on seeing how much rain we can get to put a\ndent into ongoing precipitation deficits. \n\nThe latest NBM brings chance PoPs into the area Saturday night \nwith PoPs increasing to likely along the coastal plain Sunday \nmorning. PoPs decrease Sunday afternoon as there likely will be \na lull in precipitation before the next wave crosses Sunday \nnight. This wave will send a cold front through region that will\nbring another shot of measurable rainfall. All together, \nrainfall amounts will be around 0.15 to 0.35 inches with \nsouthern NH and York County seeing potential for greater than \n0.5 inches.\n\nThe cold front will push offshore Monday morning with high \npressure building in through at least Tuesday and possibly \nWednesday. High pressure overhead Monday night will bring \nfavorable radiational cooling conditions that will allow for \nnorthern valleys to drop into the 30s for lows.",
